Fibromyalgia can cause many different symptoms. Most people have **pain**, **tiredness** and **poor sleep**, but everyone is different. Symptoms often **come and go**, and they can be much worse on some days than others.

## Pain

The main symptom is pain that is **widespread**, meaning it is in many parts of the body. It is often worse in the neck, back, shoulders and hips. People describe it as:

- a deep, constant ache
- a burning feeling
- sharp or stabbing pain
- feeling bruised all over

## Being very sensitive

With fibromyalgia, the body's pain system is on high alert. This can mean:

- things that should hurt a little hurt a lot
- things that should not hurt at all, like a hug, tight clothes or a light touch, can be painful
- you may also be sensitive to **bright lights, loud noise, smells and cold or heat**

## Extreme tiredness (fatigue)

This is not normal tiredness. It can feel like having **no energy at all**, as if you are wading through mud. Rest does not always help. Doing too much on one day can leave you exhausted for days afterwards.

## Poor sleep

Many people wake up feeling as if they have not slept at all, even after a full night in bed. Pain can wake you in the night, and some people also have **restless legs** or sleep problems like sleep apnoea.

## Fibro fog

"Fibro fog" is a common name for problems with thinking. You might:

- forget things, like appointments or why you walked into a room
- find it hard to concentrate or follow a conversation
- struggle to find the right word
- feel slow or muddled

Fibro fog is often worse when you are tired or in more pain. It can make everyday tasks, like cooking a meal or managing money, harder and less safe.

## Other common symptoms

- **stiffness**, especially in the morning or after sitting still
- **headaches** and migraines
- **irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)**, with tummy pain, bloating, diarrhoea or constipation
- **tingling, numbness or burning** in the hands and feet
- **muscle spasms** or twitches
- **feeling dizzy** or clumsy
- **needing to pee** more often
- **painful periods**
- **low mood and anxiety**

## What is a flare?

A **flare** (or flare-up) is a period when symptoms get much worse. A flare can last a few days or several weeks. Common triggers include:

- doing too much, even on a good day
- stress or upsetting news
- a poor night's sleep, or a run of them
- cold or damp weather, or sudden changes in the weather
- an infection, like a cold
- hormone changes, for example around your period or the menopause

Sometimes there is no clear trigger.

{% callout type="good" title="Tip: keep a symptom diary" %}
Write down your pain, energy and sleep each day on a scale of 0 to 10, plus what you did. After a few weeks you may spot your triggers. A diary is also strong evidence to show your GP, or to send with a benefits claim.
{% /callout %}

## What can help in a flare

- **Plan ahead.** Write a simple flare plan on a good day, listing what helps you and who can help.
- **Cut back, but keep gently moving.** Rest more, but try some gentle stretches so you do not stiffen up.
- **Use heat.** A warm bath, a heat pad or a hot water bottle can ease pain.
- **Keep to your sleep routine** as much as you can.
- **Be kind to yourself.** A flare is not your fault.
- **Talk to your GP** if flares are happening more often or lasting longer.

Read more ways to cope in our guide to [living well with fibromyalgia](/conditions/physical/fibromyalgia/living-with-fibromyalgia/).

## When to see a GP

See a GP if you have not been diagnosed and you have had widespread pain for 3 months or more. If you have been diagnosed, see a GP if your symptoms change or get much worse, or if you notice anything new.

{% callout type="danger" title="Get urgent help if" %}
You have sudden weakness or numbness in your face, arms or legs, chest pain, a very high temperature, or you lose control of your bladder or bowels. Call 999 or go to A&E.
{% /callout %}
